    <string name="help_rating_title">Rating</string>
    <string name="help_rating_text">A score from 0 to 5 combining the height that reaches the beach given the way it faces, the period, the wind and the tide that suits it. Flat: no surfable waves. Very good: worth going.</string>
    <string name="help_height_title">Wave height</string>
    <string name="help_height_text">This is the significant height: the average of the highest third of the waves. Big sets can be noticeably larger, up to almost double.</string>
    <string name="help_period_title">Period</string>
    <string name="help_period_text">Seconds between two consecutive waves. Above 10 s the swell comes from far away and arrives clean and powerful; below 8 s it\'s usually wind swell, messier and weaker.</string>
    <string name="help_swell_title">Swell</string>
    <string name="help_swell_text">Waves generated by distant storms: they arrive organised and are the best for surfing. Wind swell, on the other hand, is raised by the local wind and arrives messier.</string>
    <string name="help_wind_title">Wind type</string>
    <string name="help_wind_text">Offshore: blows from the land out to sea, holding the waves open and clean; it\'s the ideal. Cross-shore: parallel to the coast, makes them a bit messy. Onshore: blows towards the shore and makes them break early. Below 5 knots it counts as calm.</string>
    <string name="help_tide_title">Tide and coefficient</string>
    <string name="help_tide_text">The coefficient measures the size of the tide, from 20 (neap) to 120 (big spring tide). With high coefficients the water rises and falls more, and a spot can change a lot in a few hours. The best tide is the one that suits that beach most.</string>
    <string name="help_energy_title">Energy</string>
    <string name="help_energy_text">Wave power in kilowatts per metre of wave front, calculated from the height and the period (0.49 × height² × period). It sums both up in one number: 1 m at 14 s carries more energy than 1.4 m at 6 s.</string>
    <string name="help_buoy_title">Buoy and forecast</string>
    <string name="help_buoy_text">The buoy measures the real waves offshore every hour; the forecast is what the model expects. If the buoy measures more than forecast, the day may turn out better than predicted. The trend compares the latest readings with those from 6 hours earlier.</string>
